      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the \"opportunity cost\" of benefits is a significant factor, most major economic models suggest that automation and generational demographic shifts (such as the \"disappearing generation\") are likely the primary drivers of the recent participation decline.\n\nThese structural forces often outweigh the marginal impact of benefit levels because changing the supply side of the labor force is more difficult for governments to correct than simply adjusting benefit right now.\n\nConsequently, while policy can mitigate the effects of a shifting \"unwage bill\" created by technology and demographics, it is rarely the root cause of the overall trend we are seeing in the last decade.",
